1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,640
Recently, the AI world coined yet another new concept: Loop Engineering.

2
00:00:02,640 --> 00:00:06,700
Influencers are hailing it as the new era after Prompt, Context, and Harness.

3
00:00:06,700 --> 00:00:09,680
Some "expert" accounts, with a totally straight face, explain it as

4
00:00:09,680 --> 00:00:12,140
an autonomous, self-running loop system built around large models,

5
00:00:12,140 --> 00:00:14,700
upgrading AI from a one-shot response tool into a long-term autonomous agent.

6
00:00:14,700 --> 00:00:16,160
Basically, talking like a normal human is just not an option for them.

7
00:00:16,160 --> 00:00:19,060
If the earlier "Harness" was an over-packaged buzzword,

8
00:00:19,060 --> 00:00:21,660
then this time "Loop" — I honestly think it's straight-up terminology fraud.

9
00:00:21,660 --> 00:00:26,640
It's a concept forcibly cooked up by three middle-aged men and a crowd of clueless influencers.

10
00:00:26,640 --> 00:00:28,700
It's not only meaningless to ordinary people,

11
00:00:28,700 --> 00:00:30,360
it's also useless for understanding where things are actually headed.

12
00:00:30,360 --> 00:00:32,660
So let me explain it to you in plain language.

13
00:00:32,660 --> 00:00:33,700
Let's start with a quick recap.

14
00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:37,060
The first time we chatted with AI, we just asked questions, plain and simple.

15
00:00:37,060 --> 00:00:39,120
But we might not get the answer we wanted.

16
00:00:39,120 --> 00:00:42,520
Later, people found certain phrasings could improve the quality of the answers.

17
00:00:42,520 --> 00:00:45,280
The techniques of crafting and tuning these prompts

18
00:00:45,280 --> 00:00:48,920
got branded with a new term: Prompt Engineering.

19
00:00:48,920 --> 00:00:50,620
Then later, people discovered

20
00:00:50,620 --> 00:00:53,040
that adding some context in front of the prompt

21
00:00:53,040 --> 00:00:55,280
could further improve the accuracy of the answers.

22
00:00:55,280 --> 00:00:57,820
So they coined another new term: Context Engineering.

23
00:00:57,820 --> 00:00:59,180
That is, context engineering.

24
00:00:59,180 --> 00:01:00,920
Then later still, people found

25
00:01:00,920 --> 00:01:02,240
that as problems got more and more complex,

26
00:01:02,240 --> 00:01:04,880
needing long, multi-turn conversations to finish a task,

27
00:01:04,880 --> 00:01:07,460
you had to add all kinds of management and constraints around the edges

28
00:01:07,460 --> 00:01:08,940
to stop the agent from going rogue.

29
00:01:08,940 --> 00:01:11,220
So a while back they coined yet another term:

30
00:01:11,220 --> 00:01:13,320
Harness Engineering.

31
00:01:13,320 --> 00:01:15,000
As I said in an earlier video,

32
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:17,560
the term "Harness Engineering" is genuinely confusing,

33
00:01:17,560 --> 00:01:20,620
because from start to finish we've always been in the process of harnessing large models.

34
00:01:20,620 --> 00:01:23,100
There's no need to make a big show of slapping a new name on it,

35
00:01:23,100 --> 00:01:25,020
as if it were some brand-new paradigm.

36
00:01:25,020 --> 00:01:27,340
At first, people harnessed the large model directly —

37
00:01:27,340 --> 00:01:28,520
that's Prompt Engineering.

38
00:01:28,520 --> 00:01:31,360
Later, the job of harnessing the model was handed to the Agent —

39
00:01:31,360 --> 00:01:32,640
that's Context Engineering.

40
00:01:32,640 --> 00:01:35,160
Then people started studying how to harness the Agent —

41
00:01:35,160 --> 00:01:37,360
including setting yourself a set of usage rules,

42
00:01:37,360 --> 00:01:40,400
or using a dedicated framework like OpenSpec to manage it.

43
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:41,620
That's Harness Engineering.

44
00:01:41,620 --> 00:01:43,900
See? From start to finish, isn't it all just harnessing?

45
00:01:43,900 --> 00:01:46,080
When a word's scope gets too broad,

46
00:01:46,080 --> 00:01:47,680
it just becomes filler and noise.

47
00:01:47,680 --> 00:01:49,720
And Loop Engineering is even more baffling.

48
00:01:49,720 --> 00:01:52,520
Simply put, it's just calling the Agent in a loop — that's all.

49
00:01:52,520 --> 00:01:53,940
You'd think it's very high-level,

50
00:01:53,940 --> 00:01:56,020
but it doesn't actually summarize anything.

51
00:01:56,020 --> 00:01:57,260
You'd think it's very concrete,

52
00:01:57,260 --> 00:01:58,520
but then it gets crowned with some

53
00:01:58,520 --> 00:02:00,580
"next-generation programming paradigm" grand concept.

54
00:02:00,580 --> 00:02:01,800
It's even more useless than Harness.

55
00:02:01,800 --> 00:02:03,300
At this point someone's going to ask:

56
00:02:03,300 --> 00:02:05,800
why on earth did this term get so popular?

57
00:02:05,800 --> 00:02:08,580
Well, that brings us to the three men who started it all.

58
00:02:08,580 --> 00:02:09,760
The first one is Boris.

59
00:02:09,760 --> 00:02:11,540
He's the creator of Claude Code.

60
00:02:11,540 --> 00:02:13,200
In several interviews,

61
00:02:13,200 --> 00:02:15,240
he said he'd deleted his IDE,

62
00:02:15,240 --> 00:02:16,340
doesn't write a single line of code anymore,

63
00:02:16,340 --> 00:02:18,880
and has gradually cut down on talking to the Agent directly,

64
00:02:18,880 --> 00:02:20,180
using Loops more instead.

65
00:02:28,120 --> 00:02:29,800
And in a later interview,

66
00:02:29,800 --> 00:02:31,220
he went even more radical.

67
00:02:31,220 --> 00:02:33,220
Before, his wording was "more and more";

68
00:02:33,220 --> 00:02:34,820
this time, it was straight-up "everything."

69
00:02:47,300 --> 00:02:48,120
Simple, right?

70
00:02:48,120 --> 00:02:51,940
The founder is just personally promoting his own product's new feature —

71
00:02:51,940 --> 00:02:52,260
Loop.

72
00:02:52,260 --> 00:02:55,360
And the logic behind this command is very, very, very simple.

73
00:02:55,360 --> 00:02:58,120
Let me just have the founder explain it to you, and you'll get it.

74
00:03:22,100 --> 00:03:23,720
Got it?

75
00:03:23,720 --> 00:03:25,760
It's really just Claude Code's scheduled task, that's all.

76
00:03:25,760 --> 00:03:27,140
First you type "-loop",

77
00:03:27,140 --> 00:03:28,760
then you enter a time interval,

78
00:03:28,760 --> 00:03:29,680
say, one minute,

79
00:03:29,680 --> 00:03:31,620
then you enter the specific instruction,

80
00:03:31,620 --> 00:03:32,500
for example, keep it simple:

81
00:03:32,500 --> 00:03:34,160
just have it tell me the current time.

82
00:03:34,160 --> 00:03:38,420
Then Claude Code sends this prompt to itself every minute,

83
00:03:38,420 --> 00:03:39,740
and automatically presses Enter.

84
00:03:39,740 --> 00:03:40,400
That's all there is to it.

85
00:03:40,940 --> 00:03:43,560
Our domestic Agent tools from all the big companies

86
00:03:43,560 --> 00:03:45,520
have supported scheduled tasks as standard for ages.

87
00:03:45,520 --> 00:03:47,120
But hey, what can you do — Claude Code

88
00:03:47,120 --> 00:03:48,780
holds the power to define the technology.

89
00:03:48,780 --> 00:03:50,060
The second person is Peter,

90
00:03:50,060 --> 00:03:51,680
the creator of OpenCode.

91
00:03:51,680 --> 00:03:52,380
He takes one look and goes,

92
00:03:52,380 --> 00:03:52,980
"Oh man,

93
00:03:52,980 --> 00:03:55,000
the Claude Code guy dropped something new again.

94
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:57,100
Better fire off a tweet and ride the hype."

95
00:03:57,100 --> 00:03:57,900
And so,

96
00:03:57,900 --> 00:03:59,280
this time he put it even more radically.

97
00:03:59,280 --> 00:04:00,280
He flat-out told everyone:

98
00:04:00,280 --> 00:04:02,980
you shouldn't be commanding your agents anymore;

99
00:04:02,980 --> 00:04:05,760
you should design Loops to command your agents.

100
00:04:05,760 --> 00:04:06,520
Wow.

101
00:04:06,520 --> 00:04:07,220
One after another,

102
00:04:07,220 --> 00:04:07,900
each one more over-the-top,

103
00:04:07,900 --> 00:04:08,900
it's really got that flavor, alright.

104
00:04:08,900 --> 00:04:11,260
Everyone wants to be the one who names the new trend.

105
00:04:11,260 --> 00:04:12,900
But man, isn't this a bit too clickbaity?

106
00:04:12,900 --> 00:04:14,600
Of course, the even more outrageous one is the third guy:

107
00:04:14,600 --> 00:04:15,440
Google's Addy.

108
00:04:15,440 --> 00:04:17,660
His other identity is a well-known blogger.

109
00:04:17,660 --> 00:04:19,960
Previously, in web frontend and software engineering,

110
00:04:19,960 --> 00:04:21,660
he put out a ton of free works.

111
00:04:21,660 --> 00:04:22,660
And in the last couple of years,

112
00:04:22,660 --> 00:04:24,480
he's pivoted to creating AI content.

113
00:04:24,480 --> 00:04:25,860
And after Boris and Peter

114
00:04:25,860 --> 00:04:28,480
built up the momentum around the word "Loop,"

115
00:04:28,480 --> 00:04:29,420
he went and specifically wrote a

116
00:04:29,420 --> 00:04:30,640
"Loop Engineering" article,

117
00:04:30,640 --> 00:04:32,420
trying to seize the definition of this new concept.

118
00:04:32,420 --> 00:04:33,260
Wow,

119
00:04:33,260 --> 00:04:34,100
not even pretending anymore.

120
00:04:34,100 --> 00:04:36,480
This is him still smarting from not grabbing Harness back in the day.

121
00:04:36,480 --> 00:04:37,200
Anyway,

122
00:04:37,200 --> 00:04:38,940
let's give this article a quick read.

123
00:04:38,940 --> 00:04:39,700
The opening

124
00:04:39,700 --> 00:04:42,080
kicks off with an anxiety-inducing description of the trend.

125
00:04:42,080 --> 00:04:44,440
Now you know where a lot of those anxiety-words come from, right?

126
00:04:44,440 --> 00:04:46,220
These big shots are the real instigators.

127
00:04:46,220 --> 00:04:47,380
The domestic bloggers,

128
00:04:47,380 --> 00:04:49,520
at most, are just brainless middlemen

129
00:04:49,520 --> 00:04:50,480
who sped the process up.

130
00:04:50,480 --> 00:04:53,560
Next, it mentions the hype-y statements from the two big shots earlier.

131
00:04:53,560 --> 00:04:54,620
"And my goal

132
00:04:54,620 --> 00:04:56,420
is to formally take what the two of them left unsaid

133
00:04:56,420 --> 00:04:57,460
and define it for you."

134
00:04:57,460 --> 00:04:57,980
Heh heh.

135
00:04:57,980 --> 00:04:59,080
You guys make the hype,

136
00:04:59,080 --> 00:05:00,000
the credit goes to me.

137
00:05:00,340 --> 00:05:01,900
Next he starts reviewing the history,

138
00:05:01,900 --> 00:05:02,460
and along the way,

139
00:05:02,460 --> 00:05:04,640
plugs the piece he wrote when he missed the peak hype:

140
00:05:04,640 --> 00:05:05,720
Harness Engineering.

141
00:05:06,140 --> 00:05:06,620
And then

142
00:05:06,620 --> 00:05:08,980
he places Loop Engineering above that term,

143
00:05:08,980 --> 00:05:10,500
calling it the next-generation trend,

144
00:05:10,500 --> 00:05:12,760
or an application built on top of that infrastructure.

145
00:05:12,760 --> 00:05:14,320
Further down is the core part:

146
00:05:14,320 --> 00:05:16,980
he defines the five components a Loop needs.

147
00:05:16,980 --> 00:05:18,520
The first: Automations,

148
00:05:18,520 --> 00:05:19,380
automation mechanisms.

149
00:05:19,380 --> 00:05:21,540
Basically, either assign a scheduled task,

150
00:05:21,540 --> 00:05:23,420
or add a hook to trigger it automatically.

151
00:05:23,420 --> 00:05:24,660
The second: Worktrees,

152
00:05:24,660 --> 00:05:26,160
which are really just Git branches.

153
00:05:26,160 --> 00:05:27,300
The third: Skills.

154
00:05:27,300 --> 00:05:28,740
The fourth: Plugins, or MCP.

155
00:05:28,740 --> 00:05:29,880
The fifth: Sub-agents.

156
00:05:29,880 --> 00:05:30,600
Honestly,

157
00:05:30,600 --> 00:05:32,800
I think this article might be a bit rushed.

158
00:05:32,800 --> 00:05:35,520
These five terms neither form a complete set of elements,

159
00:05:35,520 --> 00:05:36,520
nor are they

160
00:05:36,520 --> 00:05:37,300
orthogonal enough to each other,

161
00:05:37,300 --> 00:05:39,540
and the scopes they describe aren't even on the same dimension.

162
00:05:39,540 --> 00:05:41,520
Next comes —

163
00:05:41,520 --> 00:05:43,220
for these concepts everyone already knows well —

164
00:05:43,220 --> 00:05:44,280
a simple description,

165
00:05:44,280 --> 00:05:45,280
like a laundry list.

166
00:05:45,280 --> 00:05:46,060
And finally,

167
00:05:46,060 --> 00:05:47,400
he gives a few common examples,

168
00:05:47,400 --> 00:05:48,500
which are also Boris's

169
00:05:48,500 --> 00:05:50,340
ancient, well-worn cases,

170
00:05:50,340 --> 00:05:51,280
nothing new.

171
00:05:51,280 --> 00:05:52,400
And at the very, very, very end,

172
00:05:52,400 --> 00:05:53,780
he doesn't forget to add a disclaimer for himself:

173
00:05:53,780 --> 00:05:55,860
"Go ahead and set up your loops.

174
00:05:55,860 --> 00:05:56,460
But also,

175
00:05:56,460 --> 00:05:58,320
don't forget that writing prompts directly

176
00:05:58,320 --> 00:05:58,940
is just as effective.

177
00:05:58,940 --> 00:06:01,020
The key is finding the right balance."

178
00:06:01,020 --> 00:06:01,640
Hold on —

179
00:06:01,640 --> 00:06:03,340
aren't tons of things in computer science

180
00:06:03,340 --> 00:06:04,420
all about trade-offs?

181
00:06:04,420 --> 00:06:05,640
That whole confident

182
00:06:05,640 --> 00:06:07,100
"the Prompt era is dead" attitude you had at the start —

183
00:06:07,100 --> 00:06:07,680
where did that go?

184
00:06:07,680 --> 00:06:09,560
What I just said might've been a bit harsh,

185
00:06:09,560 --> 00:06:11,000
so I've got to add a disclaimer for myself too.

186
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:12,280
These three men above

187
00:06:12,280 --> 00:06:14,040
are definitely big-shot-level figures.

188
00:06:14,040 --> 00:06:15,180
Us little ordinary folks

189
00:06:15,180 --> 00:06:17,000
don't really have the standing to judge what they say.

190
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:17,740
But,

191
00:06:17,740 --> 00:06:18,640
what big shots say

192
00:06:18,640 --> 00:06:20,660
tends to get a lot more attention,

193
00:06:20,660 --> 00:06:22,520
and is more easily amplified and reinterpreted,

194
00:06:22,520 --> 00:06:24,100
which then hypes up some term —

195
00:06:24,100 --> 00:06:25,660
just like when Karpathy

196
00:06:25,660 --> 00:06:27,000
offhandedly said "vibe coding."

197
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:29,280
But everyone has their own agenda.

198
00:06:29,280 --> 00:06:31,380
Boris, as the creator of Claude Code,

199
00:06:31,380 --> 00:06:33,640
obviously wants to promote his product's features.

200
00:06:33,640 --> 00:06:35,640
Peter, as the creator of OpenCode,

201
00:06:35,640 --> 00:06:36,680
after the hype died down,

202
00:06:36,680 --> 00:06:38,220
obviously wants to keep some hype alive,

203
00:06:38,220 --> 00:06:40,420
so this statement inevitably leans a little clickbaity.

204
00:06:40,420 --> 00:06:41,900
Addy, as a blogger,

205
00:06:41,900 --> 00:06:44,700
obviously wants to grab onto the hot tech term too —

206
00:06:44,700 --> 00:06:46,960
after all, each time is a once-in-a-blue-moon,

207
00:06:46,960 --> 00:06:48,200
golden chance to stay visible.

208
00:06:48,200 --> 00:06:50,140
Even someone like Karpathy,

209
00:06:50,140 --> 00:06:51,520
a very independent tech evangelist —

210
00:06:51,520 --> 00:06:53,460
I think after he joined Anthropic,

211
00:06:53,460 --> 00:06:55,580
some of his statements inevitably picked up a marketing flavor too.

212
00:06:55,580 --> 00:06:56,240
Of course,

213
00:06:56,240 --> 00:06:58,080
once you understand where a term comes from,

214
00:06:58,080 --> 00:07:00,480
it's still perfectly fine to do some thinking of our own.

215
00:07:00,480 --> 00:07:02,740
For example, people first chatted with the large model;

216
00:07:02,740 --> 00:07:04,200
actually, once the agent appeared,

217
00:07:04,200 --> 00:07:06,020
the loop idea was already there.

218
00:07:06,020 --> 00:07:07,840
That is, the agent replaces the human,

219
00:07:07,840 --> 00:07:09,260
looping in its communication with the large model,

220
00:07:09,260 --> 00:07:11,540
completing a concrete task through multi-turn dialogue.

221
00:07:11,540 --> 00:07:13,720
And now people want to find yet another program

222
00:07:13,720 --> 00:07:15,320
to loop in communication with the agent,

223
00:07:15,320 --> 00:07:17,740
to solve bigger, more persistent problems.

224
00:07:17,740 --> 00:07:19,540
You could say we've always been trying

225
00:07:19,540 --> 00:07:20,800
to find ways to automate.

226
00:07:20,800 --> 00:07:22,600
It's the same idea in other fields too.

227
00:07:22,600 --> 00:07:24,460
For example, in distributed systems,

228
00:07:24,460 --> 00:07:26,040
RAFT uses loops to do leader election;

229
00:07:26,040 --> 00:07:28,400
in K8s, loop-based monitoring

230
00:07:28,400 --> 00:07:30,700
keeps node load and various metrics stable.

231
00:07:30,700 --> 00:07:32,480
The "loop" shell itself doesn't matter.

232
00:07:32,480 --> 00:07:35,060
What matters is that the mechanism can keep running continuously,

233
00:07:35,060 --> 00:07:36,720
and auto-recover when it hits problems,

234
00:07:36,720 --> 00:07:38,440
so that, barring some extreme situation,

235
00:07:38,440 --> 00:07:40,100
it needs no human involvement at all.

236
00:07:40,480 --> 00:07:41,500
Achieving this goal

237
00:07:41,500 --> 00:07:43,880
requires careful design of algorithms and boundary conditions,

238
00:07:43,880 --> 00:07:46,140
as well as all kinds of harness configuration of the system itself.

239
00:07:46,140 --> 00:07:47,400
So for most of us,

240
00:07:47,400 --> 00:07:50,360
forget about building a system that can construct loops like this —

241
00:07:50,360 --> 00:07:52,380
we probably don't even have a single code repository,

242
00:07:52,380 --> 00:07:54,880
or have ever even used a simple prompt

243
00:07:54,880 --> 00:07:55,860
to write a little demo.

244
00:07:55,860 --> 00:07:58,400
So where's this so-called new era of Loop Engineering supposed to come from?

